        <section id="security-issues-and-threats-that-can-impact-software-development"  class="t-bg-primary">
          
            <h2>Security issues and threats that can impact software development</h2>
              <p>Scroll through the slides below to learn more about these threats.</p>

              
              
<div class="gallery-container">
  <div role="group" class="gallery no-js">
    <ul>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Social engineering
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>Social engineering is the name given to techniques used by cyber criminals to manipulate and trick people into giving out confidential information, transferring money or downloading malware.</p>
<p>Social engineers use psychology to manipulate a victim’s feelings of curiosity or fear to draw them into a trap. They often find it is easier to exploit a person’s willingness to trust rather than go to the trouble of trying to discover ways to hack their software.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cp3/cp3_u2_s6_p2_slide1.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Phishing
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>Phishing covers a broad range for cyber attacks that may come via email, phone or video conferencing. They involve social engineering to trick victims into sharing sensitive information such as passwords, usernames and even financial details for malicious reasons. Their messages pertain to be from trusted sources and ask the victim to click on a link or download an attachment, navigate a fake webpage or give verbal information. These types of attack can come about because of a lack of staff training and or inadequate firewalls. The use of fake domain names (that are similar to the actual domain name), are often used to trick taking users to fake sites. All business should make sure their staff have the up to date information about potential phishing attacks.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cp3/cp3_u2_s6_p2_slide2.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Malware
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>Malicious software or malware is any software that is designed for the purposes of intentionally causing damage or exploiting any programmable device, computer, service, network, or system.
Malicious software can include Viruses, Ransomware, Spyware,Trojans, Worms, Scareware and Adware. Some of these have already been mentioned in previous units but let's refresh our memories on what they involve.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cp3/cp3_u2_s6_p2_slide3.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          DoS and DDoS
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>A (DoS) denial of service and (DDoS) distributed denial of service attack involves computers flooding or crashing a system by creating too much internet traffic causing it to slow down and eventually stop. This can keep legitimate users from accessing a networked system, service, website, application or other resource. This type of attack uses malware and botnets. Many attackers are politically motivated and seek to damage a company’s reputation, others use it as a distraction to launch a hacking attack. Threat intelligence can be used to monitor those likely to pose a threat by checking forums etc. Filtering internet traffic and placing IP restrictions can help as this can increase the number of clients that a web server will allow.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cp3/cp3_u2_s6_p2_slide4.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Injection attacks
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>Injection attacks come in many forms but they all have one feature in common, they are designed to inject malicious code into the system to gain access to, and take control of the contents of the systems database. SQL injection flaws are introduced during the software development stage which is why it is important to consider this risk during the SSDLC. There are numerous different types of injection attack methods and there is a link below to read about these in more detail.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cp3/cp3_u2_s6_p2_slide5.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</div>

            
          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
  
